            <ArticleTitle>Copper-Catalyzed Alkylation of Aliphatic Amines Induced by Visible Light.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>17707-17710</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1021/jacs.7b09582</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Although the alkylation of an amine by an alkyl halide serves as a &quot;textbook example&quot; of a nucleophilic substitution reaction, the selective mono-alkylation of aliphatic amines by unactivated, hindered halides persists as a largely unsolved challenge in organic synthesis. We report herein that primary aliphatic amines can be cleanly mono-alkylated by unactivated secondary alkyl iodides in the presence of visible light and a copper catalyst. The method operates under mild conditions (-10 °C), displays good functional-group compatibility, and employs commercially available catalyst components. A trapping experiment with TEMPO is consistent with C-N bond formation via an alkyl radical in an out-of-cage process.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
